    "| Physical & Control Strategy | Mathematical & Numerical Properties |\n",
    "| :--- | :--- |\n",
    "| **Dynamic control strategy:** The piecewise controllers (Tasks 3 and 6) implement time-varying inlet and jacket temperatures $T_j$ and $T_0$ to place the reactor operating trajectory in the vicinity of the active temperature constraint, thereby accelerating reaction kinetics in the initial phase. | **Variance propagation (“cone” effect):** Under the constant controller of Task 2, the closed-loop dynamics are unable to adapt to realizations of the uncertainty, so the variance of the temperature process increases monotonically over time. To prevent the upper portion of this variance “cone” from exceeding the safety bound at $t = 3600$ s, the optimizer selects a comparatively low nominal temperature profile. |\n",
    "| **Active thermal throttling:** As reaction heat release and parametric uncertainty accumulate, the dynamic controllers progressively adjust the cooling temperatures in order to regulate the system close to its maximum admissible thermal capacity, while maintaining feasibility of the safety constraint. | **Enforcement of chance constraints:** The numerical schemes employed in Tasks 3 and 6 successfully enforce the prescribed 95% confidence level: the upper percentile band of the temperature trajectories intersects the 350 K ceiling around $t \\approx 1200$ s and then evolves along this boundary without exhibiting constraint violations. |\n",
    "| **Cost of static control:** Task 2 illustrates the performance degradation associated with static control under uncertainty. Constrained by the most adverse scenarios, the optimal constant input yields a median temperature trajectory that remains strictly below approximately 346 K, implying a systematic under-utilization of the available thermal capacity. | **Consistency between solvers:** The temperature trajectories, peak locations, and widths of the uncertainty bands obtained in Tasks 3 and 6 are in close agreement. This concordance indicates that the CasADi-based OCFE formulation provides an accurate discrete-time representation of the continuous-time dynamics previously simulated with SciPy. |\n",
    "| **Advantage of piecewise control:** By exploiting temporal degrees of freedom, the piecewise controllers can increase the short-term reaction rate and hence the cumulative yield, while still satisfying the safety constraints over the full horizon. This leads to a substantial performance improvement relative to the conservative static baseline. | **Objective distribution and risk–reward trade-off:** The boxplots of the objective (integral of $c_A$) evidence that piecewise strategies attain higher achievable yields (upper whiskers extending to roughly 25250), but at the expense of increased dispersion in the final performance metrics. This reflects an inherent trade-off between expected yield and robustness to uncertainty. |"
   ]
